<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ecclesiastical History of the English People</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/731">731</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Historia ecclesiastica gentis Anglorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ecclesiastical History of the English People is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> history of the English church and peoples completed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a> in 731. Running from Caesar's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to Bede's own day, it describes the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdoms and is the principal source for their early history. It popularised dating by the Anno Domini era and was translated into Old English in the late ninth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecclesiastical-history-of-the-english-people.jpg" alt="Ecclesiastical History of the English People" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bede · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beda_Petersburgiensis_f3v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> wrote the work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and is believed to have completed it in 731, when he was about 59. Its first twenty-one chapters, on the time before the mission of Augustine, were compiled from earlier writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orosius">Orosius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gildas">Gildas</a> and Prosper of Aquitaine, from the letters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a> and from legends and traditions. After 596 he used documentary sources that he took pains to obtain from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and oral testimony. His title echoes Eusebius's Historia Ecclesiastica, and like Eusebius he took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> as his model.</p><p>In 725 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> had written The Reckoning of Time, using a form of the anno Domini era devised by Dionysius Exiguus in 525, and he continued to use it throughout the History, which helped to bring about its adoption in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work has five books. The first sketches the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s invasion in 55 BCE, with a brief account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in Roman Britain, including the martyrdom of St Alban, and the mission of Augustine in 597. The second begins with the death of Gregory the Great in 604 and tells how Penda, the pagan king of Mercia, killed the newly Christian Edwin of Northumbria around 632. The third follows Christianity under the Northumbrian kings Oswald and Oswy and climaxes in the Council of Whitby. The fourth opens with the consecration of Theodore as Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> had a clear polemical and didactic purpose. He was a partisan of his native Northumbria and gave Mercia less attention. He held that the conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was the work of Irish and Italian missionaries, not of the native Britons, and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> settlement was God's punishment for their failure to evangelise and to accept the Roman date of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>. He wrote at length about that date. The parable of the sparrow, told of Edwin's council in 627, is one of its best-known passages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>About 160 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the History survive, roughly half of them on the Continent, and about 100 more hold parts of it. It was translated into Old English between the end of the 9th century and about 930; the attribution to King Alfred is no longer accepted. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> drew heavily on it for the chronological framework of its early parts. It was first printed in the later 15th century, probably at Strasbourg.</p><p>For many years early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> history was essentially a retelling of the History, but recent scholarship has looked as much at what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> did not write as at what he did.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ecclesiastical History of the English People” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bede of Jarrow (c. 673–735) wrote the <b>Ecclesiastical History of the English People</b>, completed in 731, the foundation of English historical writing, and his works on time-reckoning helped spread the practice of dating events from the birth of Christ.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…books ‘most necessary for all men to know’ to be put into English, among them Gregory the Great’s Pastoral Care, with a preface in his own name; English versions of Boethius’s Consolation of Philosophy, of Orosius’s history and of Bede’s <b>Ecclesiastical History</b> were also made in this period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Bede's <b>Ecclesiastical History of the English People</b> tells of settlers from Scythia who landed in northern Ireland and were sent on to northern Britain, taking Scoti wives.</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
